# SpreadsheetBench no_skill — the NO-CAPABILITY control for `full_verified`.
#
# A control is only a control if it differs from the thing it controls in exactly ONE respect.
# So this tier deliberately shares full_verified's dataset (verified_400), its task list, its
# committed 80/40/280 split, its 30-turn agent budget, its 8-way container concurrency and its
# scoring — and differs only in having no capability at all. Those are pinned by
# core/tests/test_no_skill_control_tier.py, because a control measured under different
# conditions is worse than no control: it yields a number that looks comparable and is not.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. Run 36175707483 scored 0.764 held-out for Gemma-4-31B-It against a SEED
# baseline of 0.632. WikiSkill (arXiv 2608.27454v1) reports 68.0 for the same model against a
# NO-SKILL baseline of 48.3. Our seed is a tuned prompt plus a task template, not an empty
# context, so our +13.2 delta is not comparable to their +19.7 — only the absolute numbers are.
# This tier supplies the missing anchor so a comparable DELTA can be stated.
#
# Same metric as full_verified. On the verified release (one graded case per task) hard == soft
# by construction, so this is a native hard score either way.
SB_SCORING=hard

# NO CAPABILITY AT ALL. Blanks seed_capability/prompt.md, and the adapter then sends NO system
# message rather than an empty one (providers differ on whether they accept an empty system
# turn, and a run that half-sends it measures neither thing). The built-in _TASK_TEMPLATE still
# supplies the task framing — so this is "no skill", not "no prompt of any kind", which is the
# closest analogue to the paper's "benchmark's default system prompt" without being identical.
# Say so when quoting the number.
#
# Mutually exclusive with SB_WARM_SEED, which run_suite.sh enforces.
SB_EMPTY_SEED=1
